{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## This ticket's headline count is now STALE — verified against live AWS tonight\n\nThe \"11 host-published alarms still carry the dead-man inversion\" claim no longer holds. Those\neleven have been fixed AND applied. Live state, read via `cloudwatch describe-alarms`:\n\n**19 alarms live, all `rebar-*`, no composite alarms. 17 of 19 are correctly configured.**\n\n`treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` (correct dead-man) — 15 alarms:\nrebar-root-disk-pressure, rebar-gerrit-data-disk-high, rebar-gerrit-data-disk-debris,\nrebar-gerrit-gate-down, rebar-mcp-serving-path-down, rebar-autodeploy-errors,\nrebar-autodeploy-mcp-mem-abort, rebar-autodeploy-mcp-retire-cap,\nrebar-autodeploy-review-interrupts-bound-exceeded,\nrebar-autodeploy-review-interrupts-signal-unavailable, rebar-gerrit-g2p-dispatch-errors,\nrebar-gerrit-voter-errors, rebar-gerrit-replication-errors, rebar-gerrit-mirror-out-of-sync,\nrebar-review-bot-merge-change-errors.\n\nLive configuration matches Terraform state exactly for every applied alarm — no drift among what\nexists. Alarm/OK actions route to the rebar-gerrit-alerts SNS topic, which has one CONFIRMED\nemail subscription. The paging path is intact.\n\n## TWO genuine remainders, not eleven\n\n**1. `rebar-bridge-run-failures` — probably a surviving instance of this bug, not an exception.**\nTerraform (monitoring_bridge.tf:40) calls it \"the DELIBERATE EXCEPTION among rebar/host alarms\".\nBut it IS a host-published `rebar/host` counter, which is exactly the class this ticket argues is\na heartbeat rather than a sparse event. Its last evaluated datapoint is **2026-09-01 09:42**, on\na 3600s/2-period window — so it is being evaluated against days-old data and reporting OK. That\nreads like the inversion rather than like an exception. The Terraform comment asserts otherwise\nand the investigator did not resolve the conflict; flagging it as the one item here that needs a\njudgement call rather than a mechanical fix.\n\n**2. `rebar-bedrock-invoke-client-errors` — defensible, leave it.** It watches an AWS-published\nBedrock metric (`InvocationClientErrors`), not a host-published one, so the publisher is not the\ndying host. monitoring_eb6e.tf:104 describes it as the \"third, outermost net\". The inversion\nargument does not apply.\n\n## A THIRD `treat_missing_data` value this ticket's sweep never covered\n\n`rebar-gerrit-ec2-instance-check` and `rebar-gerrit-ec2-system-check` are set to **`missing`** —\nneither `breaching` nor `notBreaching`. Both are evaluating against a datapoint from 2026-08-29\n17:08, the instance's launch.\n\n`missing` means **a stopped or terminated instance produces NO alarm.** That is a real hole,\nthough lower severity than the `rebar/host` class because an instance disappearing has other\ndetectors (notably rebar-gerrit-gate-down, which is correctly `breaching`).\n\nThe point for this ticket: the original sweep partitioned the world into `breaching` vs\n`notBreaching` and therefore could not see these two. If the sweep is re-run or codified as a\ncheck, it needs to enumerate ALL values of `treat_missing_data`, not just the two it was looking\nfor — otherwise the same audit will keep reporting \"clean\" over a third state it never asked\nabout.\n\n## Current alarm health — all clear\n\nAll 19 live alarms are `OK`. Zero in ALARM. Zero in INSUFFICIENT_DATA. Recent history shows the\n2026-09-02 incident and its full recovery: rebar-root-disk-pressure flapped 09-01 18:54 → 09-02\n01:45 then went OK as its publisher died (itself an illustration of why this ticket exists);\nrebar-gerrit-gate-down ALARM 02:05 → OK 07:12; rebar-autodeploy-errors ALARM 09-02 07:28 → OK\n09-03 00:25.\n\n24h counters, all below their windows: deploy_errors 1, voter_errors 2, mirror_out_of_sync 3,\nreview_interrupts 2, review_interrupts_bound_exceeded 2, **deploy_deferrals 69**. The 69\ndeferrals are the autodeploy path correctly declining to kill in-flight reviews — healthy\nbehaviour, but a high rate and worth noting as contention evidence.\n\n## Two alarms exist in code but NOT in AWS\n\n`rebar-gate-scratch-unmounted` (monitoring_autodeploy.tf:322) and `rebar-gate-scratch-disk-high`\n(:278) are absent from both AWS and Terraform state — part of the unapplied story-S1 provisioning\nrecorded on ticket aa40. Both are written `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, which is correct.\nExpect rebar-gate-scratch-unmounted to fire IMMEDIATELY once applied, because its metric is\npinned at 0. That is the alarm working.\n\nAC3 (production fault-injection) remains reserved for the human operator; nothing here changes\nthat."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788546331005362001,"uuid":"6b09cbf4-48a9-407c-8f2b-d17176921255"}